n_{\mathrm{H_2C_2O_4}}=\frac{5}{2}(0.0007899043)=0.0019747608\,\text{mol}
\]

This is in 25.00 mL = 0.02500 L, so:
\[
[\mathrm{H_2C_2O_4}]=\frac{0.0019747608}{0.02500}
=0.0789904\,\text{M}
\]

With appropriate significant figures:
\[
\boxed{0.0790\ \text{M}}
\]

We are given:

- Wavelength: \(\lambda = 780\ \text{nm} = 780\times 10^{-9}\ \text{m}\)
- Repetition rate: \(f = 100\ \text{MHz} = 1.0\times 10^8\ \text{s}^{-1}\)
- Pulse duration: \(\Delta t = 25\ \text{fs} = 25\times 10^{-15}\ \text{s}\)
- Average power: \(P_{\text{avg}} = 1.4\ \text{W}\)

We want:
1. The radiant power of each pulse, meaning the peak power during one pulse.
2. The number of photons emitted in one second.

First find the energy per pulse from the average power and repetition rate:

\[
E_{\text{pulse}}=\frac{P_{\text{avg}}}{f}
= \frac{1.4}{1.0\times 10^8}
=1.4\times 10^{-8}\ \text{J}
\]

Now the power during a pulse is the pulse energy divided by the pulse duration:

\[
P_{\text{pulse}}=\frac{E_{\text{pulse}}}{\Delta t}
=\frac{1.4\times 10^{-8}}{25\times 10^{-15}}
\]

\[
P_{\text{pulse}}=5.6\times 10^5\ \text{W}
\]

So each pulse has a radiant power of about \(5.6\times 10^5\ \text{W}\), or \(560\ \text{kW}\).

Next, photons per second. The energy of one photon is

\[
E_\gamma = \frac{hc}{\lambda}
\]

Using \(h=6.626\times 10^{-34}\ \text{J·s}\) and \(c=3.00\times 10^8\ \text{m/s}\),

\[
E_\gamma = \frac{(6.626\times 10^{-34})(3.00\times 10^8)}{780\times 10^{-9}}
\approx 2.55\times 10^{-19}\ \text{J}
\]

The number of photons emitted per second is total energy per second divided by energy per photon:

\[
N = \frac{P_{\text{avg}}}{E_\gamma}
= \frac{1.4}{2.55\times 10^{-19}}
\approx 5.5\times 10^{18}\ \text{s}^{-1}
\]

This is the photon production rate in one second.

