To relate the rate of loss of mass from a hot filament to vapour pressure, I use the Hertz–Knudsen equation for evaporation into vacuum:

\[
\frac{m}{At} = p\sqrt{\frac{M}{2\pi RT}}
\]

when \(m/(At)\) is the mass evaporated per unit area per unit time, \(p\) is vapour pressure, \(M\) is molar mass in kg mol\(^{-1}\), \(R\) is the gas constant, and \(T\) is temperature.

So first compute the mass loss rate per unit area:

\[
\frac{m}{At}=\frac{2.15\times 10^{-6}\ \text{g s}^{-1}}{5\ \text{cm}^2}
=4.30\times 10^{-7}\ \text{g cm}^{-2}\text{s}^{-1}
\]

Convert this to SI:

\[
4.30\times 10^{-7}\ \frac{\text{g}}{\text{cm}^2\text{s}}
=4.30\times 10^{-6}\ \frac{\text{kg}}{\text{m}^2\text{s}}
\]

Now use

\[
p=\left(\frac{m}{At}\right)\sqrt{\frac{2\pi RT}{M}}
\]

Given:
\[
M=183.80\ \text{g mol}^{-1}=0.18380\ \text{kg mol}^{-1},\quad
T=2800\ \text{K},\quad
R=8.314\ \text{J mol}^{-1}\text{K}^{-1}
\]

Evaluate the square-root factor:

\[
\frac{2\pi RT}{M}
=
\frac{2\pi(8.314)(2800)}{0.18380}
\approx 7.96\times 10^{5}
\]

\[
\sqrt{\frac{2\pi RT}{M}} \approx 8.92\times 10^{2}
\]

Hence

\[
p=(4.30\times 10^{-6})(8.92\times 10^{2})
\approx 3.84\times 10^{-3}\ \text{Pa}
\]

Convert to mm Hg if desired:

\[
1\ \text{mm Hg}=133.3\ \text{Pa}
\]

\[
p=\frac{3.84\times 10^{-3}}{133.3}
\approx 2.9\times 10^{-5}\ \text{mm Hg}
\]

So the vapour pressure is about \(3.8\times 10^{-3}\) Pa, or \(2.9\times 10^{-5}\) mm Hg.

We have a weak base \( \mathrm{RNH_2}\) / \( \mathrm{RNH_3OH}\)-type notation issue, but the given \(K_b\) clearly refers to a weak base of concentration \(0.01\,\text{M}\), along with added strong base \( \mathrm{NaOH}\) of \(10^{-4}\,\text{M}\).

For the weak base:
\[
\mathrm{B + H_2O \rightleftharpoons BH^+ + OH^-}, \qquad K_b=2.0\times 10^{-6}
\]

If no NaOH were present, the hydroxide from the weak base would be
\[
[\mathrm{OH^-}] \approx \sqrt{K_b C}
= \sqrt{(2\times 10^{-6})(10^{-2})}
= \sqrt{2\times 10^{-8}}
\approx 1.414\times 10^{-4}\,\text{M}
\]
which is of the same order as the added \(10^{-4}\,\text{M}\) OH\(^-\). So common-ion effect must be considered.

Let the additional OH\(^-\) produced by the weak base be \(x\). Since \(10^{-4}\,\text{M}\) OH\(^-\) is already present:
\[
[\mathrm{OH^-}] = 10^{-4}+x
\]
\[
[\mathrm{BH^+}] = x,\qquad [\mathrm{B}] \approx 0.01
\]

Apply \(K_b\):
\[
K_b=\frac{[\mathrm{BH^+}][\mathrm{OH^-}]}{[\mathrm{B}]}
=\frac{x(10^{-4}+x)}{0.01}
=2\times 10^{-6}
\]
So
\[
x(10^{-4}+x)=2\times 10^{-8}
\]
\[
x^2+10^{-4}x-2\times 10^{-8}=0
\]

Solve:
\[
x=\frac{-10^{-4}+\sqrt{(10^{-4})^2+8\times 10^{-8}}}{2}
=\frac{-10^{-4}+\sqrt{9\times 10^{-8}}}{2}
=\frac{-10^{-4}+3\times 10^{-4}}{2}
=10^{-4}
\]

Hence final hydroxide concentration:
\[
[\mathrm{OH^-}] = 10^{-4}+10^{-4}=2.0\times 10^{-4}\,\text{M}
\]

So the correct option is (b).

