Soup usually contains at least two liquid phases: a water-based broth and small amounts of melted fat or oil. These are immiscible, so they do not mix into one uniform liquid. Instead, the fat tends to collect into droplets or patches at the surface.

The reason they appear as circles is mainly surface tension and energy minimization. A droplet of one liquid surrounded by another tends to minimize interfacial area, and for a free patch or droplet that usually means a round shape. On the soup surface, the fat is also less dense than water, so it floats. The result is many nearly circular fat lenses or droplets sitting on top of the watery broth.

The size and number of circles depend on how much fat is present and how easily droplets merge or break apart. If there is more fat, droplets can grow larger or become more numerous. Stirring, pouring, or boiling can break fat into smaller droplets, increasing the number of circles. If droplets collide and coalesce, the number decreases and the average size increases.

Temperature matters because it changes viscosity and surface/interfacial tension. At higher temperature, fat is more fluid and the broth is less viscous, so droplets move and merge more easily. Heating also generally lowers surface tension, which can make droplet formation and spreading easier. On cooling, some fats become more viscous or partially solidify, which can “freeze” droplets in place and help small circles persist.

The properties of the fat and water phases are important too:
- density difference makes fat rise;
- interfacial tension between fat and water favors separation into distinct droplets;
- viscosity affects how fast droplets deform, move, and coalesce;
- composition of the fat matters because different fats melt at different temperatures and have different interfacial behaviors.

This connects directly to emulsions. An emulsion is a dispersion of one immiscible liquid in another as tiny droplets. Soup without strong emulsifiers is usually an unstable or partial emulsion, so droplets coalesce and separate into visible circles. A stable emulsion requires surfactants or emulsifiers, such as proteins, phospholipids, or detergents, which lower interfacial tension and form protective layers around droplets. That prevents coalescence, keeps droplets small, and makes the liquid look more uniform rather than showing large oil circles on the surface.

