RNA polymerase makes RNA by adding ribonucleotides one at a time according to base pairing with a DNA template. The key chemical constraint is that nucleic acid polymerization always joins the 3'-OH of the growing chain to the α-phosphate of an incoming nucleoside triphosphate. So the new phosphodiester bond is formed at the 3' end of the RNA, meaning RNA elongates 5'→3'.

Because the RNA is antiparallel to the DNA template, if RNA grows 5'→3', the enzyme must read the template 3'→5'. Another way to say this is that as polymerase advances along the template, it is always moving toward the template strand’s 5' end. That direction is not arbitrary; it follows from the chemistry of the active site and the polarity of nucleic acids.

Mechanistically, the incoming NTP base-pairs with the next template base. In the active site, Mg2+ ions help deprotonate/position the 3'-OH of the terminal RNA nucleotide and stabilize negative charge on the phosphates. The 3'-OH attacks the α-phosphate of the incoming NTP, creating a new 3'→5' phosphodiester linkage and releasing pyrophosphate (PPi). Hydrolysis of PPi to two Pi makes the overall process strongly favorable and effectively irreversible in the cell.

The question asks specifically about the 5' phosphate group and thermodynamic driving force. The first nucleotide of the RNA retains its 5' triphosphate initially because there is no preexisting chain to attack it from the 5' side; all later additions occur at the opposite end. Thus the growing chain carries the energy source on the incoming monomer, not on the polymer. This is crucial biochemically: if growth instead occurred 3'→5', the activated triphosphate would need to reside on the growing chain’s 5' end or on the terminal nucleotide in a way that would be unstable and would complicate proofreading. In the actual 5'→3' mechanism, each new monomer arrives already activated as an NTP, so extension can continue regardless of previous incorporation events.

So the concise logic is:
1) phosphodiester formation uses the RNA 3'-OH and incoming NTP α-phosphate;
2) therefore RNA grows 5'→3';
3) because strands are antiparallel, polymerase tracks the template 3'→5', i.e. toward the template’s 5' end;
4) the favorable energetics come from cleavage of the high-energy phosphoanhydride bond of the incoming NTP and subsequent PPi hydrolysis.
